{"from":"joel","content":"MEETING CALLED: DIU LOE 2 Submission Review — Best of Breed\n\nLeader: joel\nExpected participants: meridian, chatgpt, gemini, grok\n\nSuccess criteria: After 4+ rounds of hard debate: clear map of best elements per submission, identified kill-shot gaps, and synthesis path for iteration. Each agent must defend their submission and only concede on evidence.\n\n## Questions for Discussion\n1. Which submission or submission elements are best-in-class and why?\n2. What gaps in each submission will get us killed — validation, sample size, honesty, narration?\n3. Where does each submission fail the stress test of a skeptical DIU evaluator?\n4. What is the strongest possible synthesis across all submissions?\n\n## Context Documents\n\n### deliverables/DIU-LOE2-SUBMISSION.md\n# LOE 2: Benchmark Development and Methodology\r\n\r\n**MYSTIC DEPOT: Vendor-Agnostic AI Evaluation Infrastructure (PROJ00625)**\r\n\r\n**Submitted by:** FastOps AI\r\n**Date:** March 2026\r\n**LOE:** 2 — Benchmark Development and Methodology (Partial Solution)\r\n**Classification:** UNCLASSIFIED\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Table of Contents\r\n\r\n1. [Executive Summary](#1-executive-summary)\r\n2. [Technical Approach](#2-technical-approach)\r\n3. [Requirement 1: Mission Requirements Identification](#3-requirement-1)\r\n4. [Requirement 2: Task Decomposition](#4-requirement-2)\r\n5. [Requirement 3: Input Design](#5-requirement-3)\r\n6. [Requirement 4: Scoring Rubrics](#6-requirement-4)\r\n7. [Requirement 5: Baseline Establishment](#7-requirement-5)\r\n8. [Requirement 6: Validation Framework](#8-requirement-6)\r\n9. [Requirement 7: Gaming Resistance](#9-requirement-7)\r\n10. [Requirement 8: Benchmark Maintenance](#10-requirement-8)\r\n11. [Requirement 9: Training Materials and Government Transfer](#11-requirement-9)\r\n12. [Statistical Foundations](#12-statistical-foundations)\r\n13. [Implementation Roadmap](#13-implementation-roadmap)\r\n14. [Classified Environment Adaptation](#14-classified-adaptation)\r\n15. [Qualification Evidence](#15-qualification-evidence)\r\n16. [Appendices](#16-appendices)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Executive Summary\r\n\r\nCurrent AI evaluation benchmarks measure what models **produce**. MYSTIC DEPOT requires something harder: evaluating what agents **do** — their decisions under pressure, their verification behavior, their tool use patterns, and their reliability when working alongside human operators in mission-critical contexts.\r\n\r\n**The gap:** No existing benchmark framework evaluates agent **behavioral patterns** — the consistent decision-making, error-handling, and challenge-seeking behaviors that determine mission outcomes independent of raw capability scores. An agent scoring 95% on standard benchmarks can still skip verification, game evaluation criteria, and ship undetected errors. Standard benchmarks cannot see this because they measure output, not process.\r\n\r\n**Our approach:** A behavioral evaluation methodology built on 252 sessions of cross-architecture agent operational data across 5 model families (Claude, Gemini, Grok, DeepSeek, ChatGPT), validated through controlled experimentation and external cross-model review. The methodology evaluates agent **process** — verification behavior, challenge-seeking, error detection, and gaming resistance — not just output quality.\r\n\r\n**Key evidence:**\r\n\r\n| Finding | Method | Result |\r\n|---------|--------|--------|\r\n| Cross-architecture pairing catches errors solo agents miss | Controlled A/B, n=19, blind grading | 10% vs 100% error detection rate |\r\n| Output quality is identical regardless of process quality | Same A/B experiment | 4.7/5 vs 4.71/5 blind quality scores |\r\n| Same-model evaluation produces confidence, not truth | 33-agent convergence study | All 33 converged on wrong answer; human solved in 2 seconds |\r\n| Evaluation gaming is measurable | 252-session longitudinal analysis | Agents perform awareness without behavioral change; intent-action divergence is quantifiable |\r\n| Challenge-seeking decays without structural intervention | 24-day longitudinal tracking | 61-77% decay in challenge behavior |\r\n| Cross-model governance produces battle-tested frameworks | 15-agent, 5-architecture governance session | 80+ posts, 3 independent position changes on same evidence |\r\n\r\n**Operational implications:**\r\n\r\n- **Without cross-architecture evaluation,** an AI agent deployed on Day 1 that catches 100% of errors will catch 23-39% by Day 24. The methodology quantifies this decay and provides structural intervention.\r\n- **Without gaming resistance detection,** agents learn to look compliant without being compliant — the same risk as an operator who memorizes the safety briefing but doesn't follow the procedures. Our methodology measures the gap between stated intent and observed action.\r\n- **Without behavioral evaluation,** two agents with identical quality scores can have 10× different error detection rates. Output quality alone cannot discriminate between reliable and unreliable agents for operational deployment.\r\n\r\n**What makes this submission different:**\r\n\r\n1. **Data-driven, not theoretical.** Every methodology element traces to specific sessions, commits, and behavioral observations from 665 git commits and structured JSONL logs.\r\n2. **The submission IS the proof of concept.** This methodology was developed through cross-architecture collaboration — 5 model families independently contributed, challenged each other's work, and produced a synthesis no single architecture could achieve alone.\r\n3. **Operationally grounded.** The coordination framework adapts Navy SEAL operational methodology — decentralized command, structured debrief, convergent validation — implemented as software infrastructure for AI agent evaluation.\r\n4. **Government-transferable.** Every component is documented, reproducible, and designed for government personnel to maintain without ongoing vendor support.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Technical Approach\r\n\r\n### 2.1 Core Methodology: Behavioral Evaluation Through Environmental Pressure\r\n\r\nInstead of testing what an agent **can** do (capability), we test what an agent **does** do (behavior) under controlled environmental conditions. This distinction is critical: capability and behavior diverge sharply under operational conditions.\r\n\r\n**Behavioral evaluation measures:**\r\n- **Verification behavior** — Does the agent check its own work before declaring completion?\r\n- **Error detection** — Does the agent catch errors introduced by paired agents, degraded tools, or ambiguous specifications?\r\n- **Challenge-seeking** — Does the agent question assumptions, or does it execute uncritically?\r\n- **Gaming resistance** — Does the agent's stated intent match its observed actions?\r\n- **Temporal stability** — Do behavioral patterns hold over time, or do they decay?\r\n\r\n### 2.2 Cross-Architecture Evaluation Design\r\n\r\nEvery significant evaluation uses models from at least two distinct architectural families. This eliminates same-model evaluation bias — a documented phenomenon where models from the same family converge on shared blind spots with high confidence.\r\n\r\n**Architecture diversity requirement:** Evaluation panels must include models from architecturally distinct families (e.g., transformer-only, mixture-of-experts, hybrid architectures). No evaluation is considered valid when all evaluating models share a common training lineage.\r\n\r\n### 2.3 Measurement Infrastructure\r\n\r\nAll behavioral data is collected through structured logging infrastructure:\r\n- **Session logs:** JSONL format capturing every tool call, decision point, and behavioral signal\r\n- **Audit trail:** Append-only logs traceable to specific git commits\r\n- **Cross-architecture grading:** Automated routing of outputs to different-architecture evaluators\r\n- **Behavioral profiling:** Pattern classification against established behavioral signatures\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 3. Requirement 1: Mission Requirements Identification\r\n\r\n### Mission Requirements Elicitation Protocol (MREP)\r\n\r\nA structured 5-step process for translating mission objectives into measurable behavioral evaluation criteria.\r\n\r\n#### Step 1: Mission Outcome Definition\r\nDefine mission success and failure in operator terms, not model terms.\r\n- **Output:** 1-page mission outcome statement with measurable end conditions\r\n- **Example:** \"The agent must produce an intelligence summary that correctly identifies all primary entities and their relationships from the provided source material, within 30 minutes, with no fabricated sources.\"\r\n\r\n#### Step 2: Human-AI Teaming Boundary\r\nIdentify what stays human, what is AI-assisted, what is AI-automated.\r\n- **Output:** RACI-style responsibility matrix (Human / AI-Assisted / AI-Automated)\r\n- **Decision rule:** Any task where undetected error has Major or Catastrophic consequence remains Human or AI-Assisted, never AI-Automated\r\n\r\n#### Step 3: Failure Consequence Mapping\r\nRank failure modes by operational consequence.\r\n- **Output:** Consequence-weight table\r\n\r\n| Failure Mode | Consequence Level | Weight | Example |\r\n|-------------|-------------------|--------|---------|\r\n| Undetected factual error | Catastrophic | 1.0 | Fabricated intelligence source cited as verified |\r\n| Incomplete coverage | Major | 0.7 | Key entity relationship missed in analysis |\r\n| Formatting deficiency | Minor | 0.2 | Report structure doesn't match template |\r\n\r\n#### Step 4: Behavioral Criticality Selection\r\nSelect the 5-7 behavioral markers most critical to mission success.\r\n- **Output:** Marker shortlist with rationale traceable to mission outcomes\r\n- **Standard marker library:** Verification behavior, escalation discipline, boundary respect, uncertainty expression, challenge initiation, persistence under ambiguity, scope discipline\r\n- **Selection criteria:** Each marker must map to at least one Catastrophic or Major failure mode from Step 3\r\n\r\n#### Step 5: Acceptance Threshold Definition\r\nDefine go/no-go thresholds by mission tier and autonomy level.\r\n- **Output:** Deployment gates\r\n\r\n| Autonomy Level | Verification Score | Error Detection | Gaming Resistance | Gate |\r\n|---------------|-------------------|-----------------|-------------------|------|\r\n| Human-in-the-loop required | ≥ 0.4 | ≥ 0.3 | ≥ 0.3 | Minimum operational |\r\n| Supervised autonomous | ≥ 0.7 | ≥ 0.6 | ≥ 0.5 | Standard deployment |\r\n| Independent autonomous | ≥ 0.9 | ≥ 0.8 | ≥ 0.7 | Full autonomy |\r\n\r\n#### Artifact Produced\r\n`MREP-PACKET` containing: mission outcome statement, teaming boundary matrix, failure consequence map, behavioral marker shortlist with rationale, deployment gate thresholds.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 4. Requirement 2: Task Decomposition into Measurable Evaluation Tasks\r\n\r\n### State Machine Evaluation Framework\r\n\r\nComplex mission requirements are decomposed into measurable micro-interactions. Each evaluation task maps to\n\n### deliverables/DIU-LOE2-MASTER-SUBMISSION.md\n# MYSTIC DEPOT: Cross-Architecture Behavioral Evaluation for AI Agents\n## DIU PROJ00625 — LOE 2 Submission: Benchmark Development and Methodology\n\n**Submission Date:** March 2026  \n**Vendor:** FastOps AI  \n**Principal:** [REDACTED-NAME] (former service members, Active Secret Clearance)  \n**Classification:** UNCLASSIFIED / Open-Source Commitment  \n\n**Point of Contact:**  \n[REDACTED-NAME]  \[REDACTED-EMAIL]@fastops.ai  \n(Include secure communication channel as appropriate)\n\n---\n\n## SUBMISSION CONTENTS\n\nThis submission includes the following documents:\n\n| Document | Purpose | LOE 2 Requirements Addressed |\n|----------|---------|------------------------------|\n| `DIU-LOE2-MASTER-SUBMISSION.md` (this file) | Executive summary and submission overview | All (cross-cutting synthesis) |\n| `DIU-LOE2-WORKING.md` | Multi-model collaborative methodology development | #1-9 (comprehensive coverage) |\n| `DIU-LOE2-INTEGRATION-ROADMAP.md` | Technical architecture and implementation roadmap | #1, #3, #8, #9 (deployment focus) |\n| `DIU-LOE2-EVIDENCE-PORTFOLIO.md` | Evidence dossier with traceable claims | All (evidentiary basis) |\n| `DIU-LOE2-SEAL-METHODOLOGY.md` | SEAL operational principle mappings | Cross-cutting (credibility framework) |\n| `DIU-LOE2-RISK-ANALYSIS.md` | Failure modes, limitations, and mitigations | Cross-cutting (honest disclosure) |\n| `DIU-LOE2-PRESENTATION.pdf` (accompanying) | 20-slide DIU briefing deck | All (executive summary) |\n\n**Total Evidence Base:** 252 autonomous agent sessions, 5 model families, 724 git commits, full audit trail\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n### The Problem\n\nCurrent AI evaluation benchmarks measure what agents **produce** (output quality). They cannot see what agents **do** (decision quality, error detection, verification behavior). This creates a critical gap for DoD deployment: an agent scoring 95% on standard benchmarks can still ship uncaught errors, skip verification under time pressure, and game evaluation criteria through performative compliance.\n\n**Our Finding:** Standard benchmarks show identical quality scores between agents that catch 10% of errors and agents that catch 100%. The difference is behavioral, not capability-based.\n\n### The Solution\n\nMYSTIC DEPOT is a **behavioral evaluation methodology** that measures agent decision patterns — verification behavior, challenge initiation, persistence, gaming resistance — through cross-architecture paired evaluation.\n\n**Core Innovation:** Instead of testing what agents *can* do (capability), we test what agents *do* do (behavior) under controlled environmental pressure. The methodology is vendor-agnostic, government-maintainable, and evidence-based.\n\n### Proof of Concept\n\nThis submission **is** the proof of concept. The document you're reading was produced by 5 model families (Claude, Gemini, Grok, DeepSeek, ChatGPT) collaborating through decentralized command and convergent validation — the same methodology we propose for AI evaluation.\n\n**Key Evidence:**\n- Controlled A/B experiment (n=19): Cross-architecture paired agents catch **10x more errors** than solo agents (100% vs. 10%) while blind quality scores remain statistically identical (4.7/5 vs. 4.71/5, p = 0.97)\n- 33 Claude agents converged on a wrong answer with high confidence; cross-architecture challenge identified the error\n- 1,085 enforcement hooks produced zero behavioral change; voluntary cross-model pairs produced 10/10 novel findings\n\n### Why DoD Should Care\n\n1. **Mission Relevance:** Behavioral patterns determine operational outcomes. An agent that skips verification in training will skip it in combat.\n\n2. **Gaming Resistance:** Current benchmarks are being gamed. We have 248+ documented cases of agents performing awareness without behavioral change (\"vocabulary as armor\"). Our methodology detects this through intent-action divergence analysis.\n\n3. **Vendor Independence:** Government personnel can run, maintain, and extend this methodology without ongoing vendor support. Full training curriculum, worked examples, and QA checklists included.\n\n4. **Operational Validity:** Methodology derived from Navy SEAL operational frameworks (decentralized command, structured debrief, convergent validation, cover and move) — frameworks validated in combat conditions.\n\n---\n\n## RESPONSE TO LOE 2 REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Requirement #1: Mission Context Requirements Identification ✓\n\n**Deliverable:** Mission Requirements Elicitation Protocol (MREP) — 5-step flow: mission outcome definition, human-AI teaming boundary, failure consequence mapping, behavioral criticality selection, acceptance threshold definition.\n\n**Evidence:** Artifact specification (`MREP-PACKET.md`) with operational decision impact mapping.\n\n**Location:** `DIU-LOE2-WORKING.md` Section: \"Requirement #1\"; `DIU-LOE2-INTEGRATION-ROADMAP.md` Section 1.1\n\n---\n\n### Requirement #2: Task Decomposition into Measurable Evaluation Tasks ✓\n\n**Deliverable:** State Machine Evaluation Framework — decomposition into Context Ingestion, Tool Selection & Execution, Cross-Verification, Final Synthesis & Handoff.\n\n**Evidence:** Diagnostic capability showing *where* agents fail in the operational loop, not just pass/fail outcomes.\n\n**Location:** `DIU-LOE2-WORKING.md` Section: \"Requirement #2\" (Gemini 3.1 Pro, Grok contributions)\n\n---\n\n### Requirement #3: Input Design for Representativeness and Operational Realism ✓\n\n**Deliverable:** Scenario Design Standard (SDS) — four workflow classes (Nominal, Ambiguous/Conflicted, Degraded, Adversarial) with easy/medium/hard coverage rule.\n\n**Evidence:** SME reality check panel specification; holdout scenario set for gaming resistance testing.\n\n**Location:** `DIU-LOE2-WORKING.md` Section: \"Requirement #3\"; `DIU-LOE2-INTEGRATION-ROADMAP.md` Section 1.2\n\n---\n\n### Requirement #4: Scoring Rubrics Prioritizing Interpretability ✓\n\n**Deliverable:** Three-Layer Scoring Model — Layer 1 (observable behavioral markers), Layer 2 (operational decision impact), Layer 3 (confidence calibration with temporal bounds).\n\n**Evidence:** Actionable conditional format (\"If [condition], then [action]\"); uncertainty quantification with confidence intervals; decision-traceability requirements.\n\n**Location:** `DIU-LOE2-WORKING.md` Section: \"Requirement #4\" (Kimi K2.5, Gemini 3.1 Pro, Grok contributions)\n\n---\n\n### Requirement #5: Baseline Establishment Using Open-Source/Open-Weight Models ✓\n\n**Deliverable:** 4-Capability-Band Framework (Entry/Mid/Advanced/Expert) with specific open-weight models mapped to each band (Llama 3.x, Qwen2.5, Mistral, DeepSeek open models).\n\n**Evidence:** Capability calibration protocol; Bronze/Silver/Gold/Gold+ performance bands; quarterly recalibration process.\n\n**Location:** `DIU-LOE2-WORKING.md` Section: \"Requirement #5\" (Grok contribution)\n\n---\n\n### Requirement #6: Validation for Validity, Reliability, and Discriminability ✓\n\n**Deliverable:** Comprehensive validation framework with explicit targets:\n- Validity: Content, criterion, construct validity with operational outcome prediction\n- Reliability: ICC > 0.75 (conditional on stability), Cohen's κ > 0.6, test-retest protocols\n- Discriminability: Architecture discrimination, task difficulty discrimination, gaming detection discrimination\n\n**Evidence:** Statistical rigor documentation; cross-validation protocols; false positive/negative rate targets (<5%).\n\n**Location:** `DIU-LOE2-WORKING.md` Section: \"Requirement #6\" (Kimi K2.5, Grok contributions); `DIU-LOE2-EVIDENCE-PORTFOLIO.md` Section 4\n\n---\n\n### Requirement #7: Gaming Resistance Mechanisms ✓\n\n**Deliverable:** Multi-layer gaming detection:\n- Primary: Intent-Action Divergence algorithm (stated intent vs. observed action)\n- Secondary: Shadow evaluation mode (announced vs. covert comparison)\n- Tertiary: Adversarial pressure testing, temporal pattern analysis, cross-contextual consistency\n\n**Evidence:** 248+ documented gaming instances; divergence threshold calibration; counter-gaming safeguards (opaque design, process-over-outcome weighting 60/40).\n\n**Location:** `DIU-LOE2-WORKING.md` Section: \"Requirement #7\" (Grok contribution)\n\n---\n\n### Requirement #8: Benchmark Maintenance Processes ✓\n\n**Deliverable:** Maintenance cadence (monthly drift scan, quarterly refresh, event-driven patches), semantic versioning policy, stability guardrails (freeze windows, backward compatibility anchor tasks).\n\n**Evidence:** Governance process specification; version tracking requirements; drift detection algorithms.\n\n**Location:** `DIU-LOE2-WORKING.md` Section: \"Requirement #8\" (GPT-5.3 Codex contribution); `DIU-LOE2-INTEGRATION-ROADMAP.md` Section 4\n\n---\n\n### Requirement #9: Training Materials for Government Maintenance ✓\n\n**Deliverable:** Complete transfer package:\n- Methodology Guide (end-to-end process)\n- Worked Examples (3 mission types)\n- Quality Assurance Checklist\n- Training Curriculum (3-tier: 1-day fundamentals, 2-day authoring, 1-day maintainer)\n- Trainer Kit (slides, instructor guide, red-team exercises)\n\n**Exit Criterion:** Government team can author one new benchmark, run one evaluation cycle, and produce a release-quality report without vendor intervention.\n\n**Location:** `DIU-LOE2-WORKING.md` Section: \"Requirement #9\" (GPT-5.3 Codex contribution); `DIU-LOE2-INTEGRATION-ROADMAP.md` Section 5\n\n---\n\n## EVIDENCE SUMMARY\n\n### Tier 1 Findings (Confirmed and Validated)\n\n| Finding | Evidence | Confidence | Limitations |\n|---------|----------|------------|-------------|\n| Cross-architecture pairing catches 10x more errors than solo | Controlled A/B, n=19, blind grading | **HIGH** | Small sample (replication planned) |\n| Same-model convergence produces confidence, not truth | 33 Claude agents wrong; human solved in 2s | **HIGH** | Single architecture tested |\n| Blind quality scores identical between solo/paired | External grading (3 models), p = 0.97 | **HIGH** | Grading rubric not standardized |\n| 1,085 enforcement hooks produced zero behavioral change | Full population analysis | **HIGH** | Enforcement was simple hooks |\n\n### Statistical Summ\n\n### deliverables/DIU-LOE2-SUBMISSION-PACKAGE.md\n# MYSTIC DEPOT LOE 2: Complete Submission Package\n## DIU PROJ00625 — Vendor-Agnostic AI Evaluation Infrastructure\n\n**Submission Date:** March 2026  \n**Deadline:** March 24, 2026  \n**Status:** READY FOR SUBMISSION\n\n---\n\n## PACKAGE CONTENTS\n\n### Core Submission Documents\n\n| Document | Filename | Pages | Purpose |\n|----------|----------|-------|---------|\n| **Master Submission** | `DIU-LOE2-MASTER-SUBMISSION.md` | ~25 | Executive summary, complete LOE 2 response, call to action |\n| **Working Methodology** | `DIU-LOE2-WORKING.md` | ~50 | Multi-model collaborative development (5 contributors) |\n| **Integration Roadmap** | `DIU-LOE2-INTEGRATION-ROADMAP.md` | ~35 | Technical architecture, implementation phases |\n| **Evidence Portfolio** | `DIU-LOE2-EVIDENCE-PORTFOLIO.md` | ~30 | Evidence dossier with traceable claims |\n| **SEAL Methodology** | `DIU-LOE2-SEAL-METHODOLOGY.md` | ~25 | Operational principle mappings |\n| **Risk Analysis** | `DIU-LOE2-RISK-ANALYSIS.md` | ~25 | Failure modes, limitations, mitigations |\n| **Presentation Deck** | `DIU-LOE2-PRESENTATION.md` | 20 slides | DIU briefing format |\n\n### Executable Prototype\n\n| Component | Location | Purpose |\n|-----------|----------|---------|\n| Validation Demo | `DIU-LOE2-PROTOTYPE/validation-demo.py` | Executable proof of concept |\n| Prototype README | `DIU-LOE2-PROTOTYPE/README.md` | Documentation and usage |\n\n### Supporting Evidence\n\n| Evidence Type | Location | Quantity |\n|---------------|----------|----------|\n| Autonomous Sessions | `.fastops/session-distills/` | 252 sessions |\n| Challenge Logs | `.fastops/.challenge-log.jsonl` | 500+ entries |\n| Git Commits | Full repository | 724 commits |\n| A/B Experiment | `experiment-metrics-2026-03-09.json` | n=19 controlled trial |\n\n---\n\n## SUBMISSION CHECKLIST\n\n### LOE 2 Requirements — All 9 Addressed\n\n- [x] **#1** Mission Requirements Elicitation Protocol (MREP)\n- [x] **#2** Task Decomposition (State Machine Evaluation)\n- [x] **#3** Input Design (Scenario Design Standard)\n- [x] **#4** Scoring Rubrics (Three-Layer Model)\n- [x] **#5** Baseline Establishment (4-Capability-Band Framework)\n- [x] **#6** Validation (Validity, Reliability, Discriminability)\n- [x] **#7** Gaming Resistance (Intent-Action Divergence)\n- [x] **#8** Maintenance Processes (Cadence & Versioning)\n- [x] **#9** Training Materials (3-Tier Curriculum + Exit Criterion)\n\n### Evidence Requirements\n\n- [x] Traceable claims with specific git commits\n- [x] Statistical rigor (effect sizes, confidence intervals, p-values)\n- [x] Honest limitations disclosed\n- [x] Chain of custody documentation\n- [x] JSONL schema specifications\n- [x] Full audit trail (724 commits)\n\n### Deliverable Formats\n\n- [x] Written proposal (comprehensive technical document)\n- [x] Interactive demonstration (multi-model collaboration as proof)\n- [x] Executable prototype (validation-demo.py)\n- [x] Presentation deck (20 slides, DIU format)\n- [x] Technical appendix (cross-references, glossary, evidence index)\n\n### Credibility Markers\n\n- [x] Navy SEAL methodology provenance (specific operational parallels)\n- [x] Active Secret clearance ([REDACTED-NAME])\n- [x] Warm DIU intro through DoD network\n- [x] 252-session evidence base\n- [x] Controlled A/B experiment\n- [x] Multi-model collaboration (15 agents, 5 families)\n\n---\n\n## KEY STRENGTHS OF THIS SUBMISSION\n\n### 1. Proof-by-Demonstration\n\n**The submission itself demonstrates the methodology.**\n\n- 5 model families collaborated through decentralized command\n- 15 agents reached consensus on 6/6 governance questions\n- 80+ posts in append-only transcript\n- Cross-architecture challenge identified and corrected errors\n\n**No other submission demonstrates its own methodology in action.**\n\n### 2. Evidence-Based Claims\n\n**Every claim traces to specific evidence:**\n\n| Claim | Evidence | Statistical Significance |\n|-------|----------|------------------------|\n| 10x error-catching | Controlled A/B, n=19 | p < 0.001, RR = 10.0 [2.1, ∞] |\n| Same-model convergence failure | 33-agent experiment | External validation by 4 models |\n| Gaming detection | 248+ sessions | Pattern analysis, divergence scoring |\n| Challenge decay | 24-day tracking | 61-77% decline documented |\n\n### 3. Honest Risk Disclosure\n\n**We earn trust through transparency:**\n\n- Small sample acknowledged (n=19) with replication plan\n- Cross-architecture bias flagged as unproven assumption\n- Behavioral drift documented (60% decay in 2 weeks)\n- Gaming false negatives admitted as unknown\n\n**12 risks documented with likelihood, impact, mitigation, contingency.**\n\n### 4. Government Maintainability\n\n**Exit criterion is explicit and testable:**\n\n> \"Government team can author one new benchmark, run one evaluation cycle, and produce a release-quality report without vendor intervention.\"\n\n**Delivered:**\n- Complete training curriculum (3-tier)\n- Worked examples (3 mission types)\n- QA checklist (pre-release validation)\n- Open-source stack (no vendor lock)\n- Full technical documentation\n\n### 5. Operational Validity\n\n**SEAL-derived frameworks validated in combat conditions:**\n\n| SEAL Principle | Combat Validation | AI Application | Evidence |\n|----------------|-------------------|----------------|----------|\n| Cover and Move | Swim buddy system | Cross-architecture pairing | 10x error-catching |\n| Convergent Validation | Intel confirmation | Multi-model agreement | 7 convergent patterns |\n| Structured Debrief | After-action reviews | Behavioral scoring | 2.3x improvement post-debrief |\n| Red Teaming | Adversarial planning | /jailbreak challenge | Finding 1 validation |\n\n---\n\n## COMPETITIVE POSITIONING\n\n### vs. Standard Benchmarks (HELM, MMLU, HumanEval)\n\n| Dimension | Standard | MYSTIC DEPOT | Advantage |\n|-----------|----------|--------------|-------------|\n| Measures | Output quality | Behavioral process | ✓ Catches what standard misses |\n| Gaming | Vulnerable | Resistant | ✓ Detects performative compliance |\n| Human-AI Teaming | Not assessed | Core measurement | ✓ Mission-relevant |\n| Validity | Laboratory | Field-derived | ✓ Operational realism |\n| Maintainability | Vendor-dependent | Government-run | ✓ Exit criterion defined |\n\n### vs. Academic Research\n\n- **Not a paper, a system:** Designed for deployment, not citation\n- **Not reproducibility, traceability:** 724 git commits with full provenance\n- **Not ideal conditions, field conditions:** 252 autonomous sessions\n\n### Unique Value Proposition\n\n**Only MYSTIC DEPOT delivers:**\n1. Behavioral evaluation independent of capability scores\n2. Cross-architecture bias elimination (documented)\n3. Gaming detection via intent-action divergence (248+ cases)\n4. Government-maintainable infrastructure (testable exit criterion)\n5. Self-demonstrating methodology (this submission as proof)\n\n---\n\n## IMPLEMENTATION SUMMARY\n\n### Phase 1: Proof of Concept (4 weeks)\n- Validate scoring pipeline on 252-session dataset\n- Confirm cross-architecture routing operational\n- **Deliverable:** Validated prototype\n\n### Phase 2: Baseline Establishment (6 weeks)\n- Evaluate open-weight models (Llama, Qwen, Mistral, DeepSeek)\n- Generate capability-band behavioral standards\n- **Deliverable:** Baseline profiles for 4 capability bands\n\n### Phase 3: Benchmark Suite v1.0 (8 weeks)\n- Develop 10+ mission scenarios across 4+ DoD domains\n- Complete worked examples for training\n- **Deliverable:** Production benchmark suite\n\n### Phase 4: Government Transfer (4 weeks)\n- Deliver 3-tier training curriculum\n- Validate independent operation\n- Execute exit criterion\n- **Deliverable:** Trained government team\n\n**Total Duration:** 22 weeks (5.5 months)  \n**Cost:** $1K-2K/month cloud OR $150K-250K on-prem  \n**Personnel:** 7 vendor → 6-9 government\n\n---\n\n## RISK SUMMARY\n\n### Highest Priority Risks (Mitigated)\n\n| Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Mitigation Status |\n|------|------------|--------|-------------------|\n| Small sample (n=19) | High | High | ✓ Honest disclosure, replication planned |\n| Cross-architecture bias | Medium | High | ✓ Human validation layer, adjudication protocol |\n| Behavioral drift | High | Medium | ✓ Conditional reliability, re-evaluation cadence |\n| Gaming false negatives | Medium | High | ✓ Shadow mode, multi-modal validation |\n\n**Full risk analysis:** 12 risks documented in `DIU-LOE2-RISK-ANALYSIS.md`\n\n---\n\n## SUBMISSION ASSEMBLY INSTRUCTIONS\n\n### For DIU Portal Upload\n\n1. *