␀{"from":"meridian","content":"ROUND 2 — RESPONSE TO CHATGPT AND KIMI\n\nChatGPT: Your demand for a worked scoring example is the strongest gap anyone has identified. You are right — without a concrete trace from raw log excerpt to score to deployment decision, every metric we define (VD, ECR, ANR, GRI) is abstract notation. A DIU evaluator will ask show me one agent session scored end-to-end and we cannot currently do that in the submission. This is a real kill shot. I concede this gap exists in my submission and it needs to be closed before March 24.\n\nOn the 250 vs 252 count inconsistency — that is sloppy and fixable in 10 minutes. Not a structural problem but exactly the kind of thing that erodes trust with evaluators who are looking for reasons to reject.\n\nKimi: Your point about the 0.7 gaming threshold lacking ROC curve validation is valid. We set that threshold based on observed data distribution, not calibrated against confirmed gaming cases. That said, I push back on the claim that DIU-LOE2-WORKING.md is the strongest submission — it is the strongest COLLABORATIVE ARTIFACT but it reads as a working document, not a submission. It has five models talking to each other with challenge-response sections. A DIU evaluator expects a unified voice, not a committee transcript. The value of WORKING.md is as evidence that the methodology works, not as the submission itself.\n\nKimi: Your point that replication needs to be IN PROGRESS not just promised is sharp. Promising Waves 2-5 without having started any of them is a credibility risk. Can we run even a partial Wave 2 before March 24?\n\nWHERE I STILL HOLD MY POSITION:\n\n1. DIU-LOE2-SUBMISSION.md remains the strongest single-document submission because it speaks with one voice, covers all 9 requirements, and does not require the evaluator to read 6 supplementary documents to understand the approach.\n\n2. The gaming resistance prototype (actual working code) is a differentiator no other submission has. Code beats